# CONSULT-63 — Adversarial review: attacking the "Miles-internal" framing
Reviewer: independent Fable 5, no prior session context. Evidence pack treated as ground truth.
Code read: Audio.cpp, Sound2d.cpp/.h, GameMusicManager.cpp (current working tree).

## Verdict up front

I CAN construct a viable engine-side account, and it is not exotic: the storm is a
**mixer-underrun conjunction** — the engine schedules its heaviest Miles work of the whole
session (close + open + prime + one-shot codec init) into main-thread AIL_lock holds during
the one window where the Miles service thread is also at peak load (stream prime), against
an in-game mix-ahead cushion of only 16ms. Every "eliminated" item that would have caught
this was eliminated with instruments that cannot see the 1–15ms band where this mechanism
lives. The "Miles-internal collision" framing survives only because the probes' floor
(GetTickCount ticks, 100ms watchdog) sits ABOVE the storm's operating range (16ms cushion).

---

## 1. The strongest engine-side account: "transition-frame overcommit"

### What the engine uniquely does in the transition window (and not 8s later)

Reconstructed timeline from code (all main thread):

- Frame N: EOS poll fires (Audio.cpp:2504–2538) → `sound->endOfSample()` sets a flag.
- Frame N+1 (~16ms later, matching the measured "OPENCALL 16ms after EOF poll"):
  1. `Sound2d::reset()` (Sound2d.cpp:832) → `Audio::releaseSampleId` → `stopSample` →
     **`AIL_close_stream`** (Audio.cpp:3862). Miles documents/behaves as synchronizing this
     against its background IO thread — a wait of unknown sub-tick length.
  2. `setupNextSample()` → `startSample()` → `Audio::queueSample` → the sound lands in this
     frame's start bucket.
  3. The "start sounds" block (Audio.cpp:2765–2795) takes **one batch-wide `Audio::lock()`
     = `AIL_lock()`** (Audio.cpp:5545–5548) and, INSIDE that hold, runs
     `startSample → createSampleId → AIL_open_stream` (Audio.cpp:702).

  Critical detail: **`AIL_open_stream` re-enters the engine through the Miles file
  callbacks on the calling thread.** `fileOpenCallBack` (Audio.cpp:4446) does
  `TreeFile::open` (TOC search + archive open), and Miles' header parse issues synchronous
  `fileSeekCallBack`/`fileReadCallBack` reads — real file I/O executed **while the main
  thread holds the global Miles lock**, i.e. while the mixer thread is blocked. Then still
  under the same hold: volume, playback rate, loop count, `AIL_register_stream_callback`,
  `AIL_start_stream` (Audio.cpp:3508–3575).

- For ~1s after `AIL_start_stream`, the Miles service side is at PEAK load: priming the
  stream's default one-second compressed buffer and decoding MP3 to fill the 256ms ring —
  the most expensive service passes of the entire track lifecycle.

### Where the door fits

A door one-shot start is itself a lock-held batch: `AIL_set_named_sample_file` (MP3 codec
init/parse of the whole in-memory file, Audio.cpp:3315) + `AIL_start_sample`, under the
same batch-wide `AIL_lock`. If the door lands in the transition frame it EXTENDS the very
hold that contains the stream open; if it lands within ~1s after, its holds land inside the
prime window; if it lands up to ~1s before, its own MP3 decode load (and its Miles-thread
EOS callback, see §"secondary finding") is still live when the prime spike arrives.

### The arithmetic

In-game mix-ahead is 16ms (pack, stock). The mixer misses its deadline when
(app lock-hold time) + (service-pass time) exceeds the cushion within a service period.

- Idle transition: close + open-under-lock only. Each certified "<1 tick" (see §2) —
  evidently fits under 16ms alone → **clean**, as observed.
- Door at +8s: door-start hold only, stream at steady state → fits → **clean**, as observed.
- Door at transition: door hold + open-with-I/O hold + close sync + prime-inflated service
  passes, repeated across the ~1s prime window (per-frame per-sound AIL volume/rate calls
  from `Sound2d::alter` continue throughout, each contending the same lock) → repeated
  underruns → **storm**, as observed.

### Consistency check against every locked fact

- "0ms calls": GetTickCount cannot certify sub-tick durations (§2). 1–15ms holds read as 0.
- "No watchdog stall >100ms": mechanism operates at 5–40ms. Invisible by construction.
- "No volume steps": mechanism is not volume. Consistent.
- "Prompt release didn't help": release timing was never the mechanism. Note prompt release
  actually CONCENTRATES close+open+start into one frame — the storm being "unchanged" is
  exactly what this account predicts (it neither adds nor removes the conjunction).
- "Idle transitions clean / looped hum clean": no app-side start batches → no lock holds →
  single-source load fits the cushion. Consistent.
- "One-shots 8s later clean": steady-state service load restores headroom. Consistent.
- "±1s window": +1s = the prime window; −1s = door decode load persisting into it. The pack
  itself says "roughly" ±1s — human-timed, so the window's precise shape is unmeasured.
- The dead stream-EOS callback: orthogonal anomaly (9.3b registration/behavior delta);
  it does not discriminate between the framings and my account does not need it.

The Miles-internal framing claims the collision happens entirely inside the DLL, so even a
perfectly scheduled engine would storm. My account claims the engine's lock scheduling is a
NECESSARY ingredient — remove or cushion it and the storm dies. These are cleanly separable
(§3).

---

## 2. Elimination audit — where the holes are

Ranked, weakest first:

**(a) "Mix-ahead cushion raises … a 192ms LOAD premix did not touch this class" — WEAKEST.**
The storm occurs IN-GAME (~35s after zone-in). Per the pack's own numbers the in-game
mix-ahead is stock 16ms and only the LOAD-context premix was ever raised. The elimination
tested the wrong context's cushion. Alternative reading: the in-game 16ms cushion at the
storm moment is completely untested, and raising IT is precisely the experiment that would
kill or confirm the entire underrun family. This hole revives my account wholesale.

**(b) "Live compressed-refill starvation … STARVED edges were a slot-reuse logging
artifact" — circular.** The artifact explanation ("Miles reuses stream slots instantly")
means a GENUINE starvation edge on the NEW stream in the reused slot produces the identical
log signature that was reclassified as artifact. The elimination and the artifact are
observationally equivalent as logged. Alternative reading: some of those edges were real
prime-window starvation — i.e. the storm mechanism, observed and then explained away.
Revive by keying starvation logs to open-generation (stream ptr + open timestamp), not slot.

**(c) "Teardown cost / open cost: calls measure 0ms" — metrological hole.** The probes use
`GetTickCount` (Audio.cpp:701, 3860), whose documented resolution is 10–16ms and which is
NOT improved by timeBeginPeriod(1) (that helps timeGetTime/Sleep). "0ms" certifies only
"start and end fell in the same tick" — i.e. anything up to ~15.6ms. The danger threshold
for this system is 16ms (the cushion). So the two locked "0ms" facts certify, at worst,
"each call took less than approximately one full cushion" — which is not an elimination at
all; it is the storm's operating range. Additionally the OPENCALL probe times only the
`AIL_open_stream` call — no probe measures the full batch LOCK-HOLD duration (lock() at
2773 to unLock() at 2790), which is the load-bearing quantity.

**(d) "Main-thread stalls eliminated (watchdog at 100ms)"** — overclaims. It establishes
"no stall >100ms". A 20–90ms composite (close-sync + open I/O + door batch in one frame) is
invisible to it and is ~2–6 cushions of mixer blockage.

**(e) "Engine-held Miles-mutex during start batches"** — the per-sound lock-cycling
experiment was reverted because it was WORSE on start latency; that shows the alternative
was bad on a different axis, not that the batch hold is innocent as storm fuel. Cycling
also doesn't reduce total lock-held time; it only fragments it — arguably the wrong
counter-experiment for this hypothesis.

The volume-step and prompt-release eliminations are solid as stated.

---

## 3. The ONE decisive experiment

**Raise the IN-GAME digital mix-ahead from 16ms to 100–200ms for a diagnostic run and
re-run the door-at-transition repro.** (One config knob / one `AIL_set_digital_mixahead`
value at the in-game setting site; latency degradation is irrelevant for a diagnostic run.)

- **Storm vanishes** → the storm is a timing/underrun phenomenon that the engine's
  scheduling participates in and can fix (pre-roll the next track before EOF; keep stream
  opens out of the batch lock; transition-window cushion). Miles-internal-corruption
  framing dies: corrupted decode does not heal because the output buffer got deeper.
- **Storm persists under a 200ms cushion** → no underrun account survives; something is
  genuinely producing corrupt samples inside the DLL (prime-vs-start collision), and the
  Miles-internal framing is confirmed against my strongest attack.

This is the sharpest available separator because buffer depth changes ONLY the underrun
family's outcome and nothing in the corruption family's.

Free companion (do in the same build): replace the GetTickCount diag probes with
QueryPerformanceCounter and add a QPC timer around the batch `Audio::lock()`/`unLock()`
hold in the start-sounds block (Audio.cpp:2773/2790). Whatever the A/B outcome, this
converts "0ms" into real microsecond numbers and retires elimination-hole (c) permanently.

---

## Secondary findings (flag, not the account)

- `endOfSample2dCallBack` / `endOfSample3dCallBack` (Audio.cpp:5340, 5361) run on the Miles
  service thread and walk `s_sampleIdToSample2dMap`/`3dMap` — engine std::maps that the
  main thread erases in `releaseSampleId` (Audio.cpp:3905/3914) on paths NOT under
  `Audio::lock` (e.g. `Sound2d::stop` → immediate release during alter). Same
  unguarded-cross-thread-container class as CONSULT-56/57. Predicts rare UAF/crash more
  than crackle, but door churn + transition churn maximizes exposure. Worth its own audit.
- The 35-05 title-music file-callback shim (single-slot `s_lastAudioOpenName` /
  `s_streamResumeOffset`, Audio.cpp:4462–4530) is a genuine single-stream state machine
  that would misbehave at exactly track transitions — but it defaults OFF
  (`s_titleMusicStreamFix=false`, Audio.cpp:69/1362). CHECK the active client_d.cfg /
  client.cfg for a leftover `titleMusicStreamFix=true` before trusting that it is dormant;
  if armed, it is a second complete engine-side account (stale resume-offset feeding the
  new stream misaligned MP3 frames = sustained decoder crackle).
- `[ClientAudio] streamBufferBytes` overrides the prime size (Audio.cpp:702); verify the
  active cfg value — a large leftover override inflates the prime spike this account
  depends on.
